     </draw:image>
    </draw:frame><text:line-break/>And <text:span text:style-name="T27">the search is improved aswell</text:span>.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc1597_1340120094"/>Notable Removals<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc1597_1340120094"/></text:h>
   <text:h text:style-name="P42" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc1332_1340120094"/>Lucida Sans font removed<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc1332_1340120094"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32">The Lucida Sans font was removed due to licensing. The upgrade procedure will replace this font with Helvetica which is of the same type but a neo-grotesque sans-serif instead of the humanist sans-serif Lucida Sans was.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P37"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_1" text:outline-level="1"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc1415_1402974740"/>Upgrade procedure <text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc1415_1402974740"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P43">The upgrading has to be done from any <text:span text:style-name="T28">V</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">6</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T28">.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T13">1.X</text:span>. If the project has a lower version, the upgrade has to be performed stepwise following the schema </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P43">V2.1 -&gt; V2.7b -&gt; V3.3 -&gt; V3.4b -&gt; V4.0.0 -&gt; V4.1.3 →V4.2.0→V4.5.0→V4.6.0→V4.7.0→V4.8.6→(V5.0.0)→V5.1.0→V5.2.0→V5.3→V5.4→V5.5→V5.6→V5.7→V5.8→V5.9→V6.0→<text:span text:style-name="T11">V6.1.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T13">X</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32">From this point on the upgrade procedure will always be the same <text:span text:style-name="T29">with the exception being the reload part since the new pwr_upgrade.sh does not exist in the old version we are upgrading. The point of this is to make it easier to learn by heart every action needed to upgrade.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32"><text:line-break/><text:span text:style-name="T29">The upgrade procedure is split into two phases. One phase in the old “base”, that is before we switch base to the newer one. And a second phase in which we have changed base to the new version and will reload the database from the dump and apply version specific procedures.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="P44" text:outline-level="2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc1523_1340120094"/>The new tool<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc1523_1340120094"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P45">If you’re not interested in details just skip to the Upgrade Procedure section. The new pwr_upgrade.sh tool is the new go to place for upgrading projects. It’s based on Python and comes with a new structure to make it easier for us developers to create different types of required upgrade steps, whatever they might be for each version.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P45"><text:soft-page-break/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P46">The argument --<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">help</text:span> will give you a summary of the arguments and the standard steps that will always be run:<text:line-break/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P45"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr4" draw:name="Image9" text:anchor-type="char" svg:width="16.999cm" svg:height="16.11cm" draw:z-index="12"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png">
